WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.367
Who do we need to hear from to understand

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.333
how a Bible translation is serving its community?

3
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:11.567
Imagine sitting down with the people closest to the

4
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:13.700
work. What would you ask?

5
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.367
What might surprise you?

6
00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:17.467
And what could you learn by listening to them

7
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:21.233
together? That’s the idea behind 3D Review.

8
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.833
3D Review brings together three perspectives on a Bible

9
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:27.967
translation project: the translation team,

10
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:29.600
church and local leaders,

11
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.933
and the community. The translation team shares how the

12
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:34.367
work is carried out.

13
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.233
Church and local leaders share their involvement,

14
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.933
their confidence, and the impact they observe.

15
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:44.133
Community members share whether the translation is understandable and

16
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.900
meaningful in their lives.

17
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:48.567
Think of turning a gemstone in the light.

18
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.067
Each turn reveals another facet.

19
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.667
Together, those perspectives help you see more.

20
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:58.133
Survey responses become reports that bring strengths,

21
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.100
needs, and differences into view.

22
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:02.667
They give people a starting point for a better

23
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.967
conversation. Where are we hearing the same thing?

24
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:07.867
Where are experiences different?

25
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:09.633
What should we ask next?

26
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:14.033
In one pilot, community feedback highlighted concerns about audio

27
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:18.033
quality. Project leaders were already planning studio recordings.

28
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:21.533
Listening to the community helped confirm that those plans

29
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.967
addressed a real need.

30
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:27.433
3D Review doesn’t directly evaluate the translated text.

31
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:31.167
It complements existing quality-assurance work by listening to the

32
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.067
people involved. The next step begins with curiosity.

33
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:39.133
Invite the conversation. Make room for different experiences.

34
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.300
Look together at what you learn.

35
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:45.800
3D Review is being shaped through experimentation with early-adopter

36
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.000
teams. What could your team discover by listening from

37
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.433
all three perspectives?
